title The ship that had no copy
prompt raise the reds pin bound too -- and I logged in by hand in all ship folders, except petrichor still running and copal which was somehow already logged in.
think Measured the login state before answering, because the copal observation sounded like a tell rather than a curiosity, and it was.
think Found the whole pier holds exactly TWO credential files -- the host's and this tree's seeded copy -- both stamped 01:53 from my own /login, and neither touched in the 90 minutes since. Six per-folder logins wrote nothing new.
think The reason is one sentence: claude reads $HOME whatever directory it is run from. A per-folder login is the same login six times, and the last write wins. copal reading already logged in is that mechanism seen from outside -- it is the folder that followed a successful login.
obs THE SHIP THAT DID NOT DIE PROVES THE MODEL. petrichor holds no credential copy and no Jail Active line in its transcript: it runs unjailed, reading the pier's single credential live. Seven ships holding seven copies died together; the one reading the original is still sailing, and was through the outage. That is the copy model's own prediction, met without being aimed at.
obs So a real per-ship session must be made where $HOME differs, which is inside the ship's own jail: agent-jail.sh claude from that tree, /logout to drop the seeded copy, then /login. Named on the card so the next hand does not repeat six no-op logins.
obs RAISED THE REDS PIN, derived rather than picked. Measured at 24,569: header and census 3,531, six OPEN rows 21,038, mean row 3,506, %360 alone 8,213. Eight ships at one open red each times 4,096 is 32,768, plus 8,192 for the header, is 40,960.
obs WHY A RAISE RATHER THAN A FOLD, and this is the part that decided it: there was nothing left to fold. Every CLOSED row had already gone to a shelf, and reds_fold.sh refuses an open row by name at verdict=row_open -- correctly, and that refusal is the whole reason the pin is trustworthy. The night's last commit came under bound only because I shaved one row across eight passes, and a page kept under bound by attrition is a page whose rows get shorter than the truth.
obs I restored what the shaving took from %456 once the bound moved, which is the honest completion of the raise rather than a separate favour.
obs The limit is named in the law: the open set scales with the fleet AND with how long a red stays open, and the second is what actually moved tonight. A pin sized for open rows is a pin sized for how fast reds close, so the durable answer is closing them.
obs The general bound stays 24,576 and CHECKPOINTS still reads it. Two pages carry exceptions now, each derived separately, and their landing on the same number is a coincidence named rather than a shared constant.
loom pin_before=24569 pin_bound_before=24576 pin_bound_after=40960 open_rows=6 mean_open_row=3506 largest_row=8213 credential_files_on_pier=2 ships_with_copy=1 ships_unjailed_alive=1
file context/specs/20260724-132812_pin-and-ledger-living-pin-max-bytes.md the REDS exception, its derivation, and its limit
file construction/REDS.md the bound restated, %456 corroborated by petrichor, and the manual-login measurement
file construction/ITINERARY.md the raise, and the recipe for a real per-ship login
file session-logs/date/20260906/20260906-022430_the-ship-that-had-no-copy.kyri this record
recommend check-in (Keaton) -- per-ship logins want the in-jail recipe rather than the per-folder one; and %360 at 8,213 bytes has been open since 20260830, which is now the pin's largest single tenant.
